// arabic-demo — right-to-left Arabic typesetting in texish.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/arabic-demo.script
//
// Arabic adds cursive joining on top of the right-to-left layout texish already
// does for Hebrew. Each letter is drawn in one of four shapes — initial, medial,
// final or isolated — chosen from whether it joins to its neighbours. The engine
// decides each letter's form in reading order (Unicode Section 9.2 joining), then
// asks the font's GSUB table for the matching glyph: the bundled Noto Naskh Arabic
// face decomposes a dotted letter into a dotless skeleton plus a separate dot,
// shapes the skeleton, and the engine places the dot with the same GPOS anchors it
// uses for Hebrew points. The bidi reordering into visual order is unchanged.
//
// \rtl sets a right-to-left base; \ltr restores the default. \font arabic selects
// the bundled face, in a regular and a bold cut. The text below is set unpointed:
// vowel marks (harakat) are a later addition. The required lam-alef ligature is
// handled — where a lam meets an alef the two are woven into a single shape.

\use{document}

\title{Right-to-Left Arabic in \TeXish}
\author{The texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{Setting an Arabic paragraph}

With a right-to-left base direction the engine reverses each run of Arabic within a
line, reorders the words, and aligns the paragraph to the right margin. Within a
word the letters connect: the shaper picks each letter's initial, medial or final
form so the run reads as one joined stroke rather than a row of separate signs. The
line breaker is untouched — it still finds its breaks at the interword glue, in
logical order — and only the set lines are turned into visual order.

\medskip

\rtl
{\font arabic 14 regular
اللغة العربية من أقدم اللغات في العالم. تكتب من اليمين إلى اليسار، وتتصل حروفها بعضها
ببعض داخل الكلمة الواحدة. هذا النص مكتوب بخط النسخ بدون حركات، لتظهر أشكال الحروف
المتصلة بوضوح عبر عدة أسطر.
}
\ltr

\section{The lam-alef ligature}

When a lam is immediately followed by an alef the pair forms a required ligature:
the two letters are not set side by side but woven into one shape. The shaper sees
the connected lam and alef and substitutes each for the contextual variant that
nests them together. The words below each run on a lam-alef --- countries, dogs,
welcome, and the negative particle:

\medskip

\rtl
{\font arabic 16 regular
بلاد   كلاب   أهلا   ولا
}
\ltr

\section{Vowelled text}

Arabic is normally set without vowels, but they may be written as marks above and
below the letters: a fatha, kasra or damma for the short vowels, a sukun for none,
and a shadda to double a consonant. Each mark is placed by the font's anchors --- the
same mechanism that sets the Hebrew points --- so a letter carries both its
identifying dots and a vowel at once, and a shadda and a vowel stack one above the
other:

\medskip

\rtl
{\font arabic 16 regular
كَتَبَ الوَلَدُ. مُحَمَّد. الشَّمْس. بِسْمِ الرَّحِيم.
}
\ltr

\section{The word for God}

The word for God is the most frequent word in an Arabic Bible, and traditional
typography draws it as a single tightly-bound ligature when the text is pointed. The
engine forms it the way the font intends: the shadda and the superscript alef fuse
into one mark, the two lams take their connecting forms, and the whole word becomes
one calligraphic glyph. Shown first pointed, where the ligature forms, then unpointed,
where it stays as four connected letters:

\medskip

\rtl
{\font arabic 32 regular
اللّٰه \quad الله
}
\ltr

\section{A bold cut}

The bundled face ships a real bold weight, so emphasis is set by the font rather
than faked. Here a short phrase is set in bold Arabic:

\medskip

\rtl
{\font arabic 18 bold
مرحبا بالعالم
}
\ltr

\section{Bidirectional text}

Latin words and numbers keep their own left-to-right order inside a right-to-left
line: the algorithm treats each as an embedded island and only the Arabic around it
reverses. The line below names a language in Latin script and gives a version number
in digits, all within an Arabic sentence.

\medskip

\rtl
{\font arabic 14 regular
لغة البرمجة Scala تعمل على المنصة JVM ونسخة 3 منها تدعم العربية.
}
\ltr

\section{Arabic embedded in English}

The reverse case needs no change of base direction. In an ordinary left-to-right
paragraph an Arabic word — here {\font arabic 12 regular كتاب}, meaning ``book'' —
is shaped and reversed in place by the same algorithm, while the English around it
is left exactly as it was.
